WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.130
so we've been tracking the rise of Elizabeth
Warren in the 2020 Democratic primary for

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.130
awhile.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.130
Now, I already already

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:11.430
told you that, uh, Joe Biden has had a sort
of up and down polling sequence.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:15.160
Bernie Sanders had some ups and downs in his
polling.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:21.099
Elizabeth Warren on the other hand, has had
a slow but steady climb from the sort of four

00:00:21.099 --> 00:00:27.449
to 5% range to roughly 10% now, which is where
she is as of the latest polls.

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:34.680
And she's been doing it not with extraordinary
flash or viral videos or whatever else.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:39.900
She's been doing it by campaigning substantively
and doing interviews where she lays out substantive

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:46.350
policy doing town halls and talking to all
sorts of voters, including right wing voters

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.220
in red parts of the country.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.460
And I like the fact that this is what she's
doing.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.130
I don't know if it'll carry her through the
nomination, but I liked the fact that this

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:54.130
was her approach.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:01:01.500
And over the weekend she did a town hall in
Oakland, California, where 6,500 people showed

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.570
up to see Elizabeth Warren.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.619
This was at Laney College on Friday night.

00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:10.659
6,500 people waited in line outside the venue.

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:15.039
The entire thing was delayed an hour because
there were so many people trying to get in.

00:01:15.039 --> 00:01:21.990
And once again, it was a super substantive
event based on policy and ideas.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:27.509
Elizabeth Warren talked about her proposal
to end corruption, her proposals to cut money

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:32.299
in politics, proposals that would directly
help the middleclass.

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:38.710
Actually not like Trump's tax plan, uh, student
loan debt proposals, gun violence related

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:39.710
proposals.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:43.240
The only thing about it that you could criticize
is that because it was so busy and it started

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.109
so late, she ended up not taking questions
from the audience.

00:01:47.109 --> 00:01:50.460
So it ended up being more of a rally or speech
than it was a town hall.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.319
Uh, so I want to give you the pros and cons
of what's going on here with Elizabeth Warren.

00:01:54.319 --> 00:02:02.490
So as someone who has been running a substantive
issue based campaign, I love to see Elizabeth

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.109
Warren draw these types of crowds.

00:02:04.109 --> 00:02:09.530
6,500 for someone who is, yes, she's a well
known senator, but she is not the sort of

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:17.060
cult of personality, a celebrity that some
other people in politics are.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.810
I think it's absolutely fantastic.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:25.240
I've also been critical of Elizabeth Warren
before, even though I love her policy ideas

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:31.010
that she is not the most likable or the most
relatable candidate at the large level.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:36.400
I have seen her relate to people individually
in a way that is quite relatable and makes

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.790
her more likable.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:44.540
But to see a huge crowd of people fired up
by campaign finance reform or ending foreign

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:50.600
influence on our elections by foreign countries
dealing with structural inequality.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.790
It's awesome to see people fired up by that
and turning out by the thousands to hear her

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:55.970
talk about that.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:03:00.530
And so there are two ways that this could
go for Elizabeth Warren and the next few months

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:06.530
if Elizabeth Warren can keep gaining the one
to one and a half percentage points per month

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:10.130
in the polls, which is roughly what she's
been doing for the last several months, if

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:16.170
she can do that and outlast the people that
are pulling 1%, the people that are pulling

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:21.860
2% who are going to start dropping out either
by choice or by force as the Democratic National

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:27.370
Committee's primary, uh, primary debate requirements
go into force after the second debate.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:31.930
And then in the third and fourth debates,
uh, she could end up in a really strong position

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.760
by the time the fourth debate is all said
and done in October.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.730
And then it's a question of who does the progressive
vote sort of coalesce around?

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:41.350
Does it coalesce around Bernie?

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:47.270
Does it coalesce around Elizabeth Warren,
who I believe clearly are the Non Biden front

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:51.940
runners at this point in time could change,
but that's what it's looking like right now.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:57.240
Um, that's one way it could go and maybe the
debates would help her achieve that.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:01.720
On the other hand though, as I said, what
I see as Elizabeth Warren's biggest weakness

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:07.120
is she doesn't debate very well, especially
in a debate with a whole bunch of other people.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.840
I don't know the first debate, there's so
many people, they're splitting it into two,

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.240
like a first a and first be debate, and I
believe it's random.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.930
I don't know how many people will be on each
one of those debates stages.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:25.920
I don't 100% know how she gets the exact right
type of attention in that format.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.070
That will advance her campaign significantly.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:32.460
And part of it is she actually can go deep
on a lot of these issues, right?

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.139
She's got the right ideas, she has the experience.

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:37.260
She clearly has the intelligence.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:41.610
The fact that the worst Trump has been able
to do with her is color.

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:46.980
Pocahontas and talk about her native American
DNA fiasco, which was a fiasco.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.780
The way it was handled.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.260
That actually suggests to me that Republicans
are kind of scared of her when you look at

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.620
some of the issues that the country is dealing
with right now.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:02.860
And you look at Elizabeth Warren's experience,
her work in consumer and financial, uh, the

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:05.740
CFPB FPV consumer financial protection bureau.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:12.370
When you look at her work on, um, income inequality,
it lines right up with some of the biggest

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:14.620
problems that we're facing in the United States.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:19.710
And the fact that all the [inaudible] has
been able to do is say, Pocahontas suggest

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.650
to me she could do really well against Donald
Trump.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.820
And not that Bernie wouldn't as well.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:30.160
So I think it'll be very interesting to see
how does her message come through in a debate

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.139
because it does not allow, especially with
however many people is going to be eight or

00:05:34.139 --> 00:05:36.140
10 for each of the two, uh, debates.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:37.280
I don't know.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:42.940
I don't know if she can go into the depth
that is required for her to appeal to people.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:47.930
If you go to her rallies, she's going into
detail about her proposals and talking to

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:49.020
voters about them.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.600
And that is part of why she hasn't been gaining.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.070
We'll see if that translates to the debates.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:58.730
And for me it's more a question of the dynamics
of the Democratic primary and just realizing

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:04.160
that in these large, massive formats, she's
not the most relatable.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:05.670
She's not a particularly good debate.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:10.790
Or once we start the debate phase, which is
in a few weeks, it's the debates themselves

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:15.710
that are going to influence the next several
months of the campaign and probably have a

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:19.120
significant role in defining who ultimately
wins the primary.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.480
My concern is it's not Elizabeth Warren's
area of expertise, but I'm sure that she knows

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.070
this and that she's preparing in person.

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:28.730
She relates really well to people.

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:30.860
Maybe less so in debates with 10 other people.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:35.639
Uh, I, I'm curious to see what people in the
audience think about this.

00:06:35.639 --> 00:06:42.300
You know, unlike Hillary Clinton who narrowly
lost in 2016, Warren is actually further to

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:43.400
the left.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:47.010
Unlike our 2016 nominee, Hillary Clinton.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:51.580
Warren doesn't have the investigative baggage
around her.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:57.449
And the admittedly fake but impactful scandals
that Hillary had, like Ben Gazi and the email

00:06:57.449 --> 00:06:58.449
stuff.

00:06:58.449 --> 00:07:04.729
So there's every indication to me that Republicans
would rather not have Donald Trump run against

00:07:04.729 --> 00:07:05.729
Elizabeth Warren.

00:07:05.729 --> 00:07:09.770
Uh, but let me know what you think and let
me know what you believe the debates will

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:10.770
bring.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:16.139
I know that there are people who vehemently
or vehemently, um, disagree with me about

00:07:16.139 --> 00:07:18.160
what the debates will bring and how they will
affect polling, but let me know.

